WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.830
Setting up a Mac for local inference starts with a stable base system.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.833
macOS version, command line tools, package managers, Python environments, runtime installation,

00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:10.652
and model storage all matter.

00:00:10.652 --> 00:00:15.382
The exact toolchain depends on the runtime, but the operating habit is consistent:

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:20.476
make installation steps repeatable and avoid turning the daily-use system into an undocumented experiment.

00:00:20.476 --> 00:00:23.023
Package managers and Python environments are useful,

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:28.117
but they can also create confusion when dependencies are installed globally without a plan.

00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:30.300
Keep runtime-specific environments separated where practical.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.031
Document which tools are installed, where model files live, which commands start services,

00:00:35.031 --> 00:00:37.578
and which ports or interfaces are used.

00:00:37.578 --> 00:00:40.125
Small notes save large troubleshooting sessions later.

00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:44.855
Containers may be useful at a high level for packaging some application dependencies,

00:00:44.855 --> 00:00:49.221
but Apple Silicon container workflows have their own platform and performance considerations.

00:00:49.221 --> 00:00:53.224
Containers do not remove the need to understand storage, permissions, networking,

00:00:53.224 --> 00:00:54.315
and runtime compatibility.

00:00:54.315 --> 00:00:56.134
For many local LLM users,

00:00:56.134 --> 00:01:01.592
a native runtime plus clean documentation is simpler than forcing every experiment into a container.

00:01:01.592 --> 00:01:03.775
Update discipline is part of setup.

00:01:03.775 --> 00:01:08.869
A macOS update, package update, runtime update, or model manager update can change behavior.

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:10.689
Before changing a stable system,

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:14.691
record the current working state and keep a practical rollback path.

00:01:14.691 --> 00:01:19.421
The goal is to let experiments change while the base system remains understandable,

00:01:19.421 --> 00:01:23.060
recoverable, and safe to use with sensitive prompts or documents.
